/Type /StructElem
/S /H2
/T (Idea Validation: Moving from "Nice Idea" to "Viable Business" This is where we separate the daydreams from the business plans. An idea is a hobby until it makes money. The biggest mistake new entrepreneurs make is falling in love with their idea in isolation and investing time and money without first pressure-testing it in the real world. The most powerful validation tool is not a complex market research report; it is a conversation. You must leave the safety of your friends and family who will almost always praise your idea and talk to your real potential customers.)
/E (Idea Validation: Moving from "Nice Idea" to "Viable Business" This is where we separate the daydreams from the business plans. An idea is a hobby until it makes money. The biggest mistake new entrepreneurs make is falling in love with their idea in isolation and investing time and money without first pressure-testing it in the real world. The most powerful validation tool is not a complex market research report; it is a conversation. You must leave the safety of your friends and family who will almost always praise your idea and talk to your real potential customers.)
/Pg 16 0 R
/K [1 2]
>>
endobj
1164 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /H2
/T (Let's revisit our case study, Tayo's Tasty Chin-Chin. Tayo loved making chin-chin. Her friends and family always told her it was the best they'd ever tasted. The easy, and often fatal, assumption would be, "Everyone loves my chin-chin, so I will start a business." But Tayo was smarter. She spent a weekend packaging small, free samples in clear cellophane with a simple sticker. She then visited five provision shops \(aka "mama put" shops\) within a one-kilometer radius of her home. Her approach was strategic. She didn't say, "I have a business idea, will you support me?" That invites pity, not truth.)
/E (Let's revisit our case study, Tayo's Tasty Chin-Chin. Tayo loved making chin-chin. Her friends and family always told her it was the best they'd ever tasted. The easy, and often fatal, assumption would be, "Everyone loves my chin-chin, so I will start a business." But Tayo was smarter. She spent a weekend packaging small, free samples in clear cellophane with a simple sticker. She then visited five provision shops \(aka "mama put" shops\) within a one-kilometer radius of her home. Her approach was strategic. She didn't say, "I have a business idea, will you support me?" That invites pity, not truth.)
/Pg 16 0 R
/K [3 4]
>>
endobj
1165 0 obj
<<
