1296 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /H3
/T (The image of a business plan for many aspiring Naija entrepreneurs is a daunting one: a hundred-page document filled with complex financial jargon and lofty projections, seemingly written only to impress a bank manager who may never read it. This perception has paralyzed many a brilliant idea, leading countless hustlers to adopt the "just start" philosophy. They plunge headfirst into the market, fueled by passion but without a map. For a while, the energy of the launch carries them. But soon, they encounter the first major obstacle a cash flow crunch, a competitor's move, a shift in customer taste and without a plan, they are left reacting, scrambling, and often, failing.)
/E (The image of a business plan for many aspiring Naija entrepreneurs is a daunting one: a hundred-page document filled with complex financial jargon and lofty projections, seemingly written only to impress a bank manager who may never read it. This perception has paralyzed many a brilliant idea, leading countless hustlers to adopt the "just start" philosophy. They plunge headfirst into the market, fueled by passion but without a map. For a while, the energy of the launch carries them. But soon, they encounter the first major obstacle a cash flow crunch, a competitor's move, a shift in customer taste and without a plan, they are left reacting, scrambling, and often, failing.)
/Pg 27 0 R
/K [1 2]
>>
endobj
1297 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /H3
/T (Let us re-frame this entirely. In the Naija business landscape, your business plan is not a document for outsiders; it is your Battle Plan. It is the strategic blueprint you create before going to war. A general does not just tell his troops to "go and fight." He studies the terrain, understands the enemy's strength and weaknesses, plans his supply lines, and sets clear objectives. Your business is your army, your capital is your ammunition, and the market is your battlefield. Your Battle Plan is what separates a structured campaign from a chaotic, desperate skirmish.)
/E (Let us re-frame this entirely. In the Naija business landscape, your business plan is not a document for outsiders; it is your Battle Plan. It is the strategic blueprint you create before going to war. A general does not just tell his troops to "go and fight." He studies the terrain, understands the enemy's strength and weaknesses, plans his supply lines, and sets clear objectives. Your business is your army, your capital is your ammunition, and the market is your battlefield. Your Battle Plan is what separates a structured campaign from a chaotic, desperate skirmish.)
/Pg 27 0 R
/K [3 4]
>>
endobj
