/Type /StructElem
/S /H3
/T (The moment arrives in the life of every growing Naija business. The workload has surpassed what you, the founder, can handle alone. Invoices need to be sent, customers need to be attended to, products need to be packaged, and the social media page needs constant updating. The dream of being your own boss is now clashing with the reality of being the only worker. It is time to hire your first employee. This milestone is both exhilarating and terrifying. It signifies growth, but it also introduces a new layer of complexity, responsibility, and potential conflict into your business, especially in our deeply relational culture.)
/E (The moment arrives in the life of every growing Naija business. The workload has surpassed what you, the founder, can handle alone. Invoices need to be sent, customers need to be attended to, products need to be packaged, and the social media page needs constant updating. The dream of being your own boss is now clashing with the reality of being the only worker. It is time to hire your first employee. This milestone is both exhilarating and terrifying. It signifies growth, but it also introduces a new layer of complexity, responsibility, and potential conflict into your business, especially in our deeply relational culture.)
/Pg 91 0 R
/K [0 1]
>>
endobj
2155 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /H3
/T (The question that keeps many entrepreneurs awake at night is the foundational one: Who do I hire? Do I bring in my reliable cousin who needs a job? Do I partner with my best friend from university? Or do I go through the formal, and often expensive, process of finding a stranger with the right qualifications? This chapter is a deep dive into the most human aspect of your business: building a team. We will dissect the emotional and practical implications of hiring family and friends versus professionals. We will provide a structured framework for defining roles, conducting interviews, and making a hiring decision that serves the business first. Your first hire will set the cultural DNA for your company for years to come. This decision is too critical to be left to chance or sentiment.)
/E (The question that keeps many entrepreneurs awake at night is the foundational one: Who do I hire? Do I bring in my reliable cousin who needs a job? Do I partner with my best friend from university? Or do I go through the formal, and often expensive, process of finding a stranger with the right qualifications? This chapter is a deep dive into the most human aspect of your business: building a team. We will dissect the emotional and practical implications of hiring family and friends versus professionals. We will provide a structured framework for defining roles, conducting interviews, and making a hiring decision that serves the business first. Your first hire will set the cultural DNA for your company for years to come. This decision is too critical to be left to chance or sentiment.)
/Pg 91 0 R
/K [2]
>>
endobj
