3138 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /H3
/T (The story is tragically common in the Naija business scene: an entrepreneur launches with a bang. Sales are booming, money is flowing in, and the trappings of success begin to appear a new car, upgraded gadgets, frequent "business" trips. The business looks "boombastic" from the outside. Then, suddenly, the music stops. A large tax bill arrives. A key piece of equipment breaks down. A major customer delays payment. The entrepreneur looks at the business account and finds it empty. The business that was once the talk of the town is now bankrupt, its owner left with nothing but debt and regret.)
/E (The story is tragically common in the Naija business scene: an entrepreneur launches with a bang. Sales are booming, money is flowing in, and the trappings of success begin to appear a new car, upgraded gadgets, frequent "business" trips. The business looks "boombastic" from the outside. Then, suddenly, the music stops. A large tax bill arrives. A key piece of equipment breaks down. A major customer delays payment. The entrepreneur looks at the business account and finds it empty. The business that was once the talk of the town is now bankrupt, its owner left with nothing but debt and regret.)
/Pg 163 0 R
/K [3 4]
>>
endobj
3139 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /H3
/T (This catastrophic failure is rarely due to a lack of sales. It is almost always a direct result of two fatal financial sins: poor cash flow management and a failure to reinvest profits. This chapter is a deep, unflinching look at the financial discipline required to build a business that lasts. We will move beyond basic bookkeeping to master the art of cash flow forecasting, the strategy of prudent reinvestment, and the mindset shift from "owner who takes" to "steward who builds." This is about ensuring that your business's success is not a fleeting spectacle, but a sustainable legacy.)
/E (This catastrophic failure is rarely due to a lack of sales. It is almost always a direct result of two fatal financial sins: poor cash flow management and a failure to reinvest profits. This chapter is a deep, unflinching look at the financial discipline required to build a business that lasts. We will move beyond basic bookkeeping to master the art of cash flow forecasting, the strategy of prudent reinvestment, and the mindset shift from "owner who takes" to "steward who builds." This is about ensuring that your business's success is not a fleeting spectacle, but a sustainable legacy.)
/Pg 163 0 R
/K [5 6]
>>
endobj
